.Dd September 2, 2026
.Dt DIGITDISK 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm digitdisk
.Nd system snapshot, directory tree analysis and cleanup
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm digitdisk
.Op Ar subcommand
.Op Ar options
.Nm digitdisk Cm status
.Op Fl -json
.Op Fl -lang Ar language
.Op Fl -why
.Op Fl -top Ar N
.Op Fl -sample Ar MS
.Op Fl -gpu-tool
.Op Fl -live | Fl -plain
.Op Fl -interval Ar MS
.Nm digitdisk Cm analyze Ar path
.Op Fl -json
.Op Fl -lang Ar language
.Op Fl -top Ar N
.Op Fl -places Ar file
.Op Fl -no-places
.Op Fl -cross-device
.Op Fl -max-depth Ar N
.Nm digitdisk Cm clean Ar path
.Op Fl -json
.Op Fl -lang Ar language
.Op Fl -apply
.Op Fl -top Ar N
.Op Fl -trash Ar directory
.Op Fl -protect Ar what
.Op Fl -protect-file Ar file
.Op Fl -places Ar file
.Op Fl -no-places
.Op Fl -cross-device
.Op Fl -max-depth Ar N
.Nm digitdisk Cm restore Ar trash
.Op Fl -json
.Op Fl -lang Ar language
.Op Fl -dry-run
.Nm digitdisk Cm purge Ar trash
.Op Fl -json
.Op Fl -lang Ar language
.Op Fl -confirm Ar N
.Nm digitdisk Cm places
.Op Fl -json
.Op Fl -lang Ar language
.Op Fl -top Ar N
.Op Fl -places Ar file
.Op Fl -no-measure
.Nm digitdisk Cm history Ar path
.Op Fl -json
.Op Fl -lang Ar language
.Op Fl -top Ar N
.Nm digitdisk Cm run
.Op Fl -json
.Op Fl -lang Ar language
.Op Fl -plain
.Op Fl -interval Ar MS
.Op Fl -gpu-tool
.Ar command Op Ar arguments
.Nm digitdisk
.Fl c Ar command Op Ar arguments
.Nm digitdisk
.Fl -help | Fl -version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints two readings of a machine — where the disk space went and what the
machine is doing right now — and, on a separate command, removes files.
.Pp
The subcommand may be left out:
.Nm
with no arguments does what
.Nm Cm status
does, with all of its defaults.
A key where the subcommand would stand also belongs to
.Cm status ,
so
.Nm Fl -json
and
.Nm Cm status Fl -json
are the same command.
A word that matches no subcommand is not taken for a command: it is refused
with code 2 rather than read as something else.
.Pp
.Cm status ,
.Cm analyze ,
.Cm places
and
.Cm history
only read.
Three commands remove, and there are three of them:
.Cm clean
shows a plan and without
.Fl -apply
opens no file for writing,
.Cm restore
puts back what was moved,
.Cm purge
erases.
.Pp
What is removed is exactly what the decision layer gave the verdict
.Dq Removable :
not what resembles it, and not what matched a pattern.
.Dq Removable
is the core's own name for that verdict — an identifier
rather than an English word — and under
.Fl -json
it travels as
.Dq МожноУбрать .
.Pp
Moving to the trash frees no space.
.Fl -apply
is
.Xr rename 2
inside the same file system: the files stay on the disk under another name,
and the number
.Cm clean Fl -apply
prints is what was moved, not what was freed.
Only
.Cm purge
frees space, and that is irreversible.
.Pp
The language is asked once.
On the first run at a terminal
.Nm
asks which language to write in and stores the answer in
.Pa ~/.digitable/digitdisk/settings.conf .
Where it is not a terminal — a pipe, a script, CI — or where
.Fl -json
is given, nothing is asked and nothing is stored: the language comes from
.Ev LC_ALL ,
.Ev LC_MESSAGES
and
.Ev LANG ,
and a locale that is unset or names no language of the two means English.
.Fl -lang
overrides both the stored choice and the locale for one run,
.Ev DIGITDISK_LANG
for a session.
.Pp
.Fl -json
is not translated: its keys and its machine values stay byte for byte what
they were, because scripts read them.
The Russian words that travel in JSON as values — the class
.Dq Кэш ,
the verdict
.Dq МожноУбрать ,
the texts of a refusal — are identifiers of the decision
layer, not output text.
.Sh SUBCOMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width "history <path>XX"
.It Cm status
A snapshot of the system: kernel and release, uptime, load, memory, processes,
disks, network, temperature.
The sources are the platform's own:
.Pa /proc
and
.Pa /sys
on Linux,
.Xr sysctl 3
and libSystem on macOS.
What the system does not publish is printed as a dash, not as a zero, and is
named in the
.Dq NOT READ
section; why, under
.Fl -why .
At a terminal a live screen is drawn; into a pipe or a file the same snapshot
is printed as text.
.It Cm analyze Ar path
A walk of the tree at
.Ar path
through
.Xr lstat 2 :
directories by size and the largest files.
Symbolic links are not followed, without
.Fl -cross-device
the walk does not cross a file system boundary, and what cannot be read is
counted and stepped over.
A hard link is counted once.
.It Cm clean Ar path
The cleanup plan for
.Ar path :
which files, how many bytes, and by which rule of the core they are marked
.Dq Removable .
Without
.Fl -apply
nothing is touched.
With
.Fl -apply
what is marked is moved to the trash and a journal is written.
.It Cm restore Ar trash
The trash named by
.Ar trash
goes back where it came from, by its own journal.
It needs no key and overwrites nothing: a name already taken is an entry
.Dq not restored ,
not a replaced file.
.It Cm purge Ar trash
Erasing the trash named by
.Ar trash
— the one irreversible action.
Without
.Fl -confirm
it prints the plan and the number that has to be named.
.It Cm places
The known-places directory: what
.Nm
knows about particular caches and how much of that is on this machine.
The directory is data: it is edited without a rebuild.
.It Cm history Ar path
How past cleanups under the root
.Ar path
ended: when, how many files, how many bytes are lying in trashes, how much
was restored and how much was erased.
Nothing is remembered between runs — all of it is read
from the journals of the trashes themselves.
.It Cm run Ar command
Runs somebody else's command and shows what it cost: a status line on the
last row of the terminal while it works, and a two-line summary after.
.Pp
The WHOLE TREE of processes is counted, not the direct child alone:
a parallel
.Xr make 1 ,
npm and a Go build spawn dozens, and
measuring one child would show almost nothing.
There are three ways to count, and the summary names
the one that answered: a control group of our own
(cgroup v2 — the kernel counts, exactly, losing nothing
between two samples), a walk over
.Pa /proc
by parent links (an approximation by sampling — a process
that lives and dies between two samples is not seen, and
a sum of RSS counts a shared page once per process
holding it), and the total from
.Xr wait4 2
(the exact CPU time of the command and of everything it
waited for, and the exact peak of the largest single process).
.Pp
Standard output belongs to the command.
Neither the status line, nor the summary, nor
.Fl -json
is written there: everything the wrapper prints goes to standard error, and
.Pp
.Dl $ digitdisk run make | tee log
.Pp
puts into the file exactly what
.Ql make | tee log
would put there.
There is no status line at all when standard error is not a terminal; a
full-screen program
.Pq Xr vim 1 , Xr ssh 1 , Xr less 1
takes the terminal for itself, and the line goes away and comes back on its
own.
.Pp
The wrapper's own keys go BEFORE the command, the command's keys after it:
.Nm Cm run Fl -json Cm ls Fl -json
gives the first
.Fl -json
to the wrapper and the second to
.Xr ls 1 .
The short spelling of the same thing is
.Fl c .
One word holding spaces and shell metacharacters is handed to the shell from
.Ev SHELL
.Pq or Pa /bin/sh ;
anything else is started directly, with no shell standing between the
wrapper and the command.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--protect-file fileXX"
.It Fl -json
Machine-readable output.
Every subcommand takes it.
Under
.Fl -json
the list of work of
.Cm clean
travels whole and is not cut by
.Fl -top .
.It Fl -lang Ar language
.Cm ru
or
.Cm en
for one run; it overrides both the stored choice and the locale.
Every subcommand takes it.
.It Fl -top Ar N
How many lines in the lists: 10 for
.Cm status ,
15 for
.Cm analyze
and
.Cm clean ,
40 for
.Cm places ,
20 for
.Cm history .
.Ar N
= 0 means no limit.
The totals and the breakdown by class are counted over the whole plan and do
not depend on
.Fl -top .
.It Fl -why
.Cm status :
instead of the snapshot — what was not measured and why.
.It Fl -sample Ar MS
.Cm status :
the window over which CPU load is measured, milliseconds, 200 by default.
.It Fl -gpu-tool
.Cm status , run :
allow an outside program to be run (
.Xr nvidia-smi 1 ) for the video-card numbers the driver publishes in no file
at all.
By default digitdisk only reads files; under every card it says where its
numbers came from.
.Cm run
asks exactly one thing: how much video memory the command's own processes
hold.
Per-process card load is published nowhere by the driver, and instead of an
invented number the summary says so in words: the load of the whole card is
not the load of the command while anything else is computing beside it.
.It Fl -live
.Cm status :
the live screen.
Without a terminal it is an error, not a quiet fall back to text.
.It Fl -plain
.Cm status :
one snapshot as text, even when the output goes to a terminal.
.Cm run :
no status line, even where there is a terminal.
.It Fl -interval Ar MS
.Cm status :
how often the live screen is refreshed, milliseconds, 2000 by default.
.Cm run :
how often the status line is refreshed, 1000 by default.
The tree of processes is read more often than the line is drawn, and the
reading thins itself out when it turns out to cost more than a twentieth of
its own period; what it came to is said in the summary and in
.Fl -json .
.It Fl c Ar command
The short spelling of
.Cm run :
everything AFTER
.Fl c
belongs to the command, everything before it to the wrapper.
A word that is not a subcommand ends the search for
.Fl c :
.Nm Cm clean Pa ~ Fl c
is a key of
.Cm clean ,
not a wrapper.
.It Fl -cross-device
.Cm analyze , clean :
walk into other file systems that are mounted there.
By default the walk stops at the boundary.
.It Fl -max-depth Ar N
.Cm analyze , clean :
the depth limit of the walk, 0 means no limit.
.It Fl -places Ar file
.Cm analyze , clean , places :
a known-places directory of one's own instead of the built-in one.
.It Fl -no-places
.Cm analyze , clean :
judge by the signs alone, without the directory.
.It Fl -no-measure
.Cm places :
do not measure the places that were found, only name them.
.It Fl -apply
.Cm clean :
move what is marked to the trash rather than only showing the plan.
.It Fl -trash Ar directory
.Cm clean :
another trash instead of
.Pa <root>/.digitdisk-trash .
It has to lie inside the root of the cleanup.
.It Fl -protect Ar what
.Cm clean :
leave alone.
.Ar what
is either a path or a class, written
.Dq разряд: Ns Ar class ;
the prefix and the class names are the core's own and are not translated.
The key may be repeated.
The protect list only subtracts: it moves an entry from the plan into
.Dq PROTECTED
and never adds a file to the plan.
.It Fl -protect-file Ar file
.Cm clean :
the protect list from a file instead of
.Pa ~/.digitable/digitdisk/protect.conf .
.It Fl -dry-run
.Cm restore :
show what would come back, and put nothing back.
.It Fl -confirm Ar N
.Cm purge :
confirm the erasing of exactly
.Ar N
files.
.Ar N
has to match the number of files in the trash.
.It Fl -help , Fl h , Cm help
Help: the subcommands and the keys on one page.
.It Fl -version , Fl V , Cm version
The version, the build hash, the toolchain and the decision layer of this
binary.
.El
.Sh LIVE SCREEN
.Nm
and
.Nm Cm status
draw a live screen when the output goes to a terminal.
There are eleven sections, and the first of them \(em
.Sy COMMANDS
\(em does not read but starts: it is the list of subcommands, the same list
.Fl -help
is built from, and the line chosen in it is run.
.Pp
.Cm analyze
and
.Cm clean
ask for a path on an input line that offers the current directory and completes
on
.Ic Tab ;
the walk is visible from the first second and
.Ic q
stops it.
Cleaning from the screen goes the way
.Cm clean
goes: the decision layer's verdict, the plan, and the exact number of files
typed out by hand.
.Cm purge
is started from no screen under any condition \(em it is the one irreversible
step, and it is typed out in the shell in full.
A subcommand run from the screen prints as it always prints, and
.Ic Enter
brings the status screen back: the program does not restart in between.
.Pp
The keys:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "PgUp, PgDnXX" -compact
.It Ic \(<-\ \(->, Tab
the previous and the next section
.It Ic 1\(en9
that section at once; 1 is
.Sy COMMANDS
.It Ic \(ua\ \(da, PgUp, PgDn
scrolling a long section
.It Ic \(ua\ \(da, 1\(en7 Pq in COMMANDS
choose a subcommand
.It Ic Enter Pq in COMMANDS
run the chosen one; Esc goes back to OVERVIEW
.It Ic p , Space
pause
.It Ic r
measure now
.It Ic l
switch the language and store the new choice
.It Ic \&?
straight to the COMMANDS section
.It Ic q , Esc
quit
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "XDG_CONFIG_HOMEXX"
.It Ev DIGITDISK_LANG
.Cm ru
or
.Cm en
for a session: it overrides the stored choice and the locale, and
.Fl -lang
overrides it.
.It Ev LC_ALL , LC_MESSAGES , LANG
The language of the machine, in that order, when nothing was chosen.
A locale that is unset or names no language of the two means English.
.It Ev DIGITDISK_PALETTE
The palette of the live screen:
.Cm carbon
(the default),
.Cm paper ,
.Cm signal .
.It Ev NO_COLOR
Set, it turns colour off.
.It Ev TERM
.Cm dumb
or an empty value — no live screen, text is printed.
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the older
.Pa digitdisk/places.conf
and
.Pa digitdisk/protect.conf
are looked for;
.Pa ~/.config
by default.
.It Ev XDG_CACHE_HOME , XDG_DATA_HOME
Where the known-places directory aims its
.Dq cache
and
.Dq data
anchors;
.Pa ~/.cache
and
.Pa ~/.local/share
by default.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "<root>/.digitdisk-trash/<label>/journal.jsonXX"
.It Pa <root>/.digitdisk-trash/<label>/
The trash that
.Cm clean Fl -apply
makes.
It is always inside the root of the cleanup; the label is a UTC time of the
form
.Pa 2026-09-02T12-00-00Z .
.It Pa <root>/.digitdisk-trash/<label>/files/
The moved files, each under its own path relative to the root.
.It Pa <root>/.digitdisk-trash/<label>/journal.json
The journal of the trash: where each file was taken from, its size and its
fingerprint, what was restored and what was erased.
.Cm restore ,
.Cm purge
and
.Cm history
read it; without it a directory is no trash of
.Nm .
.It Pa ~/.digitable/digitdisk/settings.conf
The language, stored after the question was answered by hand.
Nothing else is written here.
.It Pa ~/.digitable/digitdisk/places.conf
A known-places directory of one's own.
With no such file the directory built into the binary is used;
.Cm places
prints which of the two was taken.
.It Pa ~/.digitable/digitdisk/protect.conf
The protect list: lines of the form
.Pa путь|<path>|<why>
and
.Pa разряд|<class>|<why> .
.It Pa ~/.config/digitdisk/places.conf , ~/.config/digitdisk/protect.conf
The older home, still read when the new one holds no such file.
Nothing is copied out of it: the file is moved by hand, when it suits.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width "0XX" -compact
.It 0
The command did its work.
.It 1
A failure while running: no such path, a directory that is no trash,
.Fl -confirm
naming the wrong number.
.It 2
An unknown subcommand or a key that was not parsed.
.It 126
.Cm run :
the command is there and will not run.
.It 127
.Cm run :
there is no such command.
.El
.Pp
Under
.Cm run
the exit code is the COMMAND's, not the wrapper's: 0 and 1 mean what they
mean for it, and the wrapper can be put into a script without changing it.
A command killed by a signal is repeated on the wrapper with the same signal,
so the shell sees exactly what it would have seen without one.
Ctrl-C reaches the command by itself: it stays in
the wrapper's process group, and the terminal signals both.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The live screen of the state:
.Pp
.Dl $ digitdisk
.Pp
The same snapshot, for a machine:
.Pp
.Dl $ digitdisk --json | jq .memory
.Pp
The same snapshot, in the other language:
.Pp
.Dl $ digitdisk --lang ru
.Pp
Where the space went in the home directory, in twenty lines:
.Pp
.Dl $ digitdisk analyze ~ --top 20
.Pp
The cleanup plan; nothing is touched:
.Pp
.Dl $ digitdisk clean ~
.Pp
The move to the trash, with two exemptions:
.Pp
.Dl $ digitdisk clean ~ --protect ~/projects --protect разряд:сборка --apply
.Pp
Second thoughts:
.Pp
.Dl $ digitdisk restore ~/.digitdisk-trash/2026-09-02T12-00-00Z
.Pp
Freeing the space — irreversible, and the number is said out loud:
.Pp
.Dl $ digitdisk purge ~/.digitdisk-trash/2026-09-02T12-00-00Z
.Dl $ digitdisk purge ~/.digitdisk-trash/2026-09-02T12-00-00Z --confirm 128
.Pp
What was removed under this root and how much of it is still in the trashes:
.Pp
.Dl $ digitdisk history ~
